theory Java_Parser
  imports "../Isabelle_Lex-Yacc/LexYacc" "Java_Ast"
begin

section\<open>Lexer and Parser for Java\<close>

text\<open>
  This theory contains the lexer and the parser of Java, generated by
  \<open>ml_lex_yacc [state]\<close> (the re-entrant variant of the library: every parse has
  its own state object), and \<^verbatim>\<open>Java_Parse\<close>, the parse driver. What is Java, and where
  does its grammar come from?

  \<^bold>\<open>Java has an official grammar.\<close> \<^emph>\<open>The Java Language Specification, Java SE 25 Edition\<close>
  (\<^url>\<open>https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se25/html/\<close>) repeats the syntax of the language
  in its chapter 19, and this front-end follows that chapter for the whole of the language of
  Java 25 as it stands in the specification - that is, with everything that became final up to
  Java 25, and without the preview features. What the grammar covers:

  \<^item> \<^bold>\<open>compilation units\<close>: ordinary ones (package, imports, classes), modular ones
    (\<open>module-info.java\<close>: \<open>open module\<close>, \<open>requires [transitive|static]\<close>, \<open>exports\<close>, \<open>opens\<close>, \<open>uses\<close>,
    \<open>provides ... with\<close>), and compact ones (JEP 512: methods and fields at the top level, instance
    \<open>main\<close>), with the imports of single types, on-demand, static and (JEP 511) whole modules;
  \<^item> \<^bold>\<open>declarations\<close>: classes, interfaces (with \<open>default\<close> and private methods), enums (with
    constant bodies), records (with compact constructors), annotation interfaces (with
    \<open>default\<close> values), sealed hierarchies (\<open>sealed\<close>, \<open>non-sealed\<close>, \<open>permits\<close>), generic classes and
    methods, initializers, receiver parameters, and the constructors with a \<open>this(...)\<close> or
    \<open>super(...)\<close> call anywhere in their body (JEP 513);
  \<^item> \<^bold>\<open>statements\<close>: all of them, with local classes, records, enums and interfaces, \<open>var\<close>, labels,
    \<open>try\<close> with resources, \<open>switch\<close> (old-style and with rules), \<open>yield\<close>, \<open>assert\<close>, and the unnamed
    variable \<open>_\<close> (JEP 456);
  \<^item> \<^bold>\<open>expressions\<close>: the operators with their precedences, lambdas, method references,
    \<open>switch\<close> expressions, \<open>instanceof\<close> with type and record patterns (nested, with \<open>var\<close> and
    \<open>_\<close>), pattern labels with guards (\<open>case Foo f when ...\<close>), casts (also to intersection
    types), array and class creation, text blocks, and every form of number literal;
  \<^item> \<^bold>\<open>annotations\<close> everywhere the specification allows them.

  \<^bold>\<open>The layout stream.\<close> Java cannot be read by an LALR(1) parser over the tokens of its
  lexer. A \<open><\<close> can begin type arguments (\<open>List<String> x\<close>, \<open>Map.Entry<K,V>::getKey\<close>) or be a
  comparison; a \<open>(\<close> can begin a lambda (\<open>(a, b) -> ...\<close>), a cast (\<open>(String) x\<close>), a record pattern
  (\<open>case Point(int x, int y) ->\<close>) or be a plain parenthesis; \<open>>>\<close> can close two lists of type
  arguments; a name can be a parameter of a lambda (\<open>x -> ...\<close>) or a switch constant; and
  \<open>var\<close>, \<open>yield\<close>, \<open>record\<close>, \<open>sealed\<close>, \<open>permits\<close>, \<open>when\<close>, \<open>module\<close>, \<open>open\<close>, \<open>requires\<close>, \<open>exports\<close>,
  \<open>opens\<close>, \<open>uses\<close>, \<open>provides\<close>, \<open>to\<close>, \<open>with\<close> and \<open>transitive\<close> are keywords or names by their
  surroundings. As javac does, a small stream between the lexer and the parser
  (\<open>Java_Parse.java_stream\<close>) looks ahead in the token list, scans a type without building any
  tree, and hands the parser tokens that say which it is: \<open>TLT\<close> and \<open>TGT\<close> for the brackets of type
  arguments and parameters (\<open>>>\<close> and \<open>>>>\<close> are split), \<open>LAMLP\<close>, \<open>CASTLP\<close> and \<open>RECLP\<close> for the
  parenthesis that opens the parameters of a lambda, a cast or a record pattern, \<open>LAMID\<close> for the
  parameter of a lambda without parentheses, \<open>EMPTYBRK\<close> for \<open>[]\<close>, and the keyword tokens of the
  contextual keywords. The grammar itself is then an ordinary context-free one, with no
  conflict left.

  \<^bold>\<open>Comments\<close> are \<open>//\<close> to the end of the line and \<open>/* ... */\<close> (they do not nest); they may
  contain antiquotations \<open>//@ tag[navi](level) \<open>body\<close>\<close>, attached to the node of the next
  real token, as in the other front-ends.

  \<^bold>\<open>Known limitations of the lexer and the stream.\<close> The lexer works on UTF-8 bytes; an
  identifier is a letter, \<open>_\<close> or \<open>$\<close> followed by letters, digits, \<open>_\<close> and \<open>$\<close>, where every non-ASCII
  byte counts as a letter. Unicode escapes (\<open>\\u0041\<close>) are not translated before lexing: they are
  recognised inside character and string literals and comments, which is where they occur in
  practice. The stream decides by the tokens only (it does not know types): in an argument list,
  \<open>f(a < b, c > d)\<close> is read as a call with two comparisons, as javac does, but a declaration
  such as \<open>Map<A, B> m\<close> next to it is read as a declaration.
\<close>
ML\<open>
(* Everything the lexer and the grammar actions need to remember during one
   parse lives in one state object, created per parse ("init") and handed to
   the lexer (ML-Lex %arg) and the parser (ML-Yacc %arg) by "ml_lex_yacc
   [state]" - nothing is shared between parses.
     base        - the library's own part: source, context, position table
     pending     - comments lexed since the previous real token
     attached    - the comments that have been attached to a token position
     layout      - the layout recorder, see "Java_Layout"
     antiq_*     - the antiquotation scanner (see the lexer): nesting depth of
                   the cartouche being skipped, which comment state to return
                   to, the most recent tag, the body text so far and its start
                   position
     comment_depth, bodyless_ok
                 - whether a block comment is open; whether a bodyless
                   antiquotation tag is allowed in the current comment
     lex_error   - the first lexical error (unterminated comment, string or
                   antiquotation): the lexer cannot raise it itself, as the
                   parse's error callback decides how an error is reported
                   (loudly, or silently for the "_reject" commands), so it is
                   recorded here and reported by "Java_Parse.parse" when the
                   parse is over *)
structure Java_State = struct
  type comments = Position.T Java_Ast.comment list
  type layout = {nl: int, start: int, stop: int, kind: string}
  val layout_init : layout = {nl = 0, start = 0, stop = 0, kind = ""}

  type T = {base : Lex_yacc_state.T,
            pending : comments Unsynchronized.ref,
            attached : (Position.T * comments) list Unsynchronized.ref,
            layout : layout Unsynchronized.ref,
            antiq_depth : int Unsynchronized.ref,
            antiq_from_block : bool Unsynchronized.ref,
            antiq_tag : (string * Java_Ast.navi list * int * Position.T)
              Unsynchronized.ref,
            antiq_buf : string list Unsynchronized.ref,
            antiq_start : Position.T Unsynchronized.ref,
            comment_depth : int Unsynchronized.ref,
            bodyless_ok : bool Unsynchronized.ref,
            comment_start : int Unsynchronized.ref,
            lex_error : (string * Position.T) option Unsynchronized.ref}

  fun init ctxt source : T =
    {base = Lex_yacc_state.init ctxt source,
     pending = Unsynchronized.ref [],
     attached = Unsynchronized.ref [],
     layout = Unsynchronized.ref layout_init,
     antiq_depth = Unsynchronized.ref 0,
     antiq_from_block = Unsynchronized.ref true,
     antiq_tag = Unsynchronized.ref ("", [], 0, Position.none),
     antiq_buf = Unsynchronized.ref [],
     antiq_start = Unsynchronized.ref Position.none,
     comment_depth = Unsynchronized.ref 0,
     bodyless_ok = Unsynchronized.ref false,
     comment_start = Unsynchronized.ref 0,
     lex_error = Unsynchronized.ref NONE}
end

(* The comments/antiquotations collected while lexing, attached to whichever
   real token follows (as in C11_Comments / Occam2_Comments / Scala3_Comments).
   *)
structure Java_Comments = struct
  fun push (st : Java_State.T) c = #pending st := !(#pending st) @ [c]

  fun push_raw st (text, pos) =
    if List.all Char.isSpace (String.explode text) then ()
    else push st (Java_Ast.Raw_txt [(text, pos)])

  fun push_antiquotation st tag navi level cartouche =
    push st (Java_Ast.Antiquotation (tag, navi, level, cartouche))

  fun attach_pending (st : Java_State.T) (p : Position.T) =
    (case !(#pending st) of
       [] => ()
     | cs => (#pending st := []; #attached st := (p, cs) :: !(#attached st)))

  (* Non-destructive on purpose, for the reason given at "C11_Comments.claim".
     *)
  fun claim (st : Java_State.T) (p : Position.T) : Position.T
    Java_Ast.comment list =
    (case AList.lookup (op =) (!(#attached st)) p of NONE => [] | SOME cs => cs)

  fun mk_nodeInfo st (claimed_at : Position.T)
    (report_pos : Position.T) : Position.T Java_Ast.nodeInfo =
    (case claim st claimed_at of
       [] => Java_Ast.OnlyPos report_pos
     | cs => Java_Ast.NodeInfo (cs, report_pos))
end

(* Layout bookkeeping shared between the lexer actions and the stream wrapper.
   The lexer only ever records facts here; it never emits a separator token
   itself:
     nl     - the number of newlines lexed since the previous real token (also
              those inside general comments, as in the Go specification)
     start/stop - character offsets of the newest real token
     kind   - the newest token's kind: its name in the grammar ("IDENT",
              "RBRACE", ...) *)
structure Java_Layout = struct
  fun get (st : Java_State.T) = !(#layout st)

  fun newline (st : Java_State.T) =
    let val {nl, start, stop, kind} = get st
    in #layout st := {nl = nl + 1, start = start, stop = stop, kind = kind} end

  fun token (st : Java_State.T) (start, stop, kind) =
    let val {nl, ...} = get st
    in #layout st := {nl = nl, start = start, stop = stop, kind = kind} end

  fun clear_newlines (st : Java_State.T) =
    let val {start, stop, kind, ...} = get st
    in #layout st := {nl = 0, start = start, stop = stop, kind = kind} end
end
\<close>
SML_import \<open>structure Java_Ast = struct open Java_Ast end\<close>
SML_import \<open>structure Java_State = struct open Java_State end\<close>
SML_import \<open>structure Java_Comments = struct open Java_Comments end\<close>
SML_import \<open>structure Java_Layout = struct open Java_Layout end\<close>

SML_import \<open>structure Java_Ast = struct open Java_Ast end\<close>
SML_import \<open>structure Java_State = struct open Java_State end\<close>
SML_import \<open>structure Java_Comments = struct open Java_Comments end\<close>
SML_import \<open>structure Java_Layout = struct open Java_Layout end\<close>

ml_lex_yacc [verbose, state] "Java" where

lex_user_declarations\<open>
(* The lexer's and the parser's argument is the per-parse state of
   "Java_State" (the lexer rules and the grammar actions call it "st");
   "init" creates it, "base" is its part the library functions work on. *)
type arg = Java_State.T
fun init ctxt source : arg = Java_State.init ctxt source
fun base (st : arg) : Lex_yacc_state.T = #base st

fun gp (st : arg) yypos = Lex_yacc_state.get_pos (base st) yypos
fun report (st : arg) x = Lex_yacc_state.report_token (base st) x

fun antiq_depth (st : arg) = #antiq_depth st
fun antiq_from_block (st : arg) = #antiq_from_block st
fun antiq_tag (st : arg) = #antiq_tag st
fun antiq_buf (st : arg) = #antiq_buf st
fun antiq_start (st : arg) = #antiq_start st
fun comment_depth (st : arg) = #comment_depth st
fun bodyless_ok (st : arg) = #bodyless_ok st
fun comment_start (st : arg) = #comment_start st

(* Every real token goes through one of the constructors below, which, before
   reporting the token's markup, (1) let the comments gathered since the
   previous token attach themselves to this one, and (2) tell the layout
   recorder where this token starts and stops and what kind of token it is.
   Value-carrying tokens get their true start/end span as position. *)
fun stok st kind markup (yypos, yytext, cons) =
    (Java_Comments.attach_pending st (gp st yypos);
     Java_Layout.token st (yypos, yypos + String.size yytext, kind);
     tok (base st) (yypos, yytext, markup, kind, "", cons))

fun stok_val st kind markup (yypos, yytext, cons, value) =
    let
      val _ = Java_Comments.attach_pending st (gp st yypos)
      val _ = Java_Layout.token st (yypos, yypos + String.size yytext, kind)
      val p = gp st yypos
      val p' = gp st (yypos + String.size yytext)
      val _ = report st (yypos, String.size yytext, markup, kind, "")
    in cons (value, p, p') end



(* Java's reserved words (JLS 3.9), the literals "true", "false" and "null", and "_" (a keyword
   since Java 9); every other word is an identifier. The contextual keywords of Java 25 (var,
   yield, record, sealed, permits, when, module, open, requires, exports, opens, uses, provides,
   to, with, transitive) are identifiers for the lexer: the layout stream (Java_Parse.java_stream)
   decides from their surroundings whether one of them is a keyword here. They are therefore
   not reported when they are lexed - the stream reports them, as a keyword or as a name. *)
val contextual_words =
    ["var", "yield", "record", "sealed", "permits", "when", "module", "open", "requires",
     "exports", "opens", "uses", "provides", "to", "with", "transitive"]

fun quiet_ident st (yypos, yytext) =
    let
      val _ = Java_Comments.attach_pending st (gp st yypos)
      val _ = Java_Layout.token st (yypos, yypos + String.size yytext, "IDENT")
    in Tokens.IDENT (yytext, gp st yypos, gp st (yypos + String.size yytext)) end

fun word_tok st (yypos, yytext) =
    let
      fun kw kind cons = stok st kind Markup.keyword1 (yypos, yytext, cons)
      fun lit kind cons = stok st kind Markup.keyword3 (yypos, yytext, cons)
    in
      case yytext of
        "abstract" => kw "ABSTRACT" Tokens.ABSTRACT
      | "assert" => kw "ASSERT" Tokens.ASSERT
      | "boolean" => kw "BOOLEAN" Tokens.BOOLEAN
      | "break" => kw "BREAK" Tokens.BREAK
      | "byte" => kw "BYTE" Tokens.BYTE
      | "case" => kw "CASE" Tokens.CASE
      | "catch" => kw "CATCH" Tokens.CATCH
      | "char" => kw "CHAR" Tokens.CHAR
      | "class" => kw "CLASS" Tokens.CLASS
      | "const" => kw "CONST" Tokens.CONST
      | "continue" => kw "CONTINUE" Tokens.CONTINUE
      | "default" => kw "DEFAULT" Tokens.DEFAULT
      | "do" => kw "DO" Tokens.DO
      | "double" => kw "DOUBLE" Tokens.DOUBLE
      | "else" => kw "ELSE" Tokens.ELSE
      | "enum" => kw "ENUM" Tokens.ENUM
      | "extends" => kw "EXTENDS" Tokens.EXTENDS
      | "final" => kw "FINAL" Tokens.FINAL
      | "finally" => kw "FINALLY" Tokens.FINALLY
      | "float" => kw "FLOAT" Tokens.FLOAT
      | "for" => kw "FOR" Tokens.FOR
      | "goto" => kw "GOTO" Tokens.GOTO
      | "if" => kw "IF" Tokens.IF
      | "implements" => kw "IMPLEMENTS" Tokens.IMPLEMENTS
      | "import" => kw "IMPORT" Tokens.IMPORT
      | "instanceof" => kw "INSTANCEOF" Tokens.INSTANCEOF
      | "int" => kw "INT" Tokens.INT
      | "interface" => kw "INTERFACE" Tokens.INTERFACE
      | "long" => kw "LONG" Tokens.LONG
      | "native" => kw "NATIVE" Tokens.NATIVE
      | "new" => kw "NEW" Tokens.NEW
      | "package" => kw "PACKAGE" Tokens.PACKAGE
      | "private" => kw "PRIVATE" Tokens.PRIVATE
      | "protected" => kw "PROTECTED" Tokens.PROTECTED
      | "public" => kw "PUBLIC" Tokens.PUBLIC
      | "return" => kw "RETURN" Tokens.RETURN
      | "short" => kw "SHORT" Tokens.SHORT
      | "static" => kw "STATIC" Tokens.STATIC
      | "strictfp" => kw "STRICTFP" Tokens.STRICTFP
      | "super" => kw "SUPER" Tokens.SUPER
      | "switch" => kw "SWITCH" Tokens.SWITCH
      | "synchronized" => kw "SYNCHRONIZED" Tokens.SYNCHRONIZED
      | "this" => kw "THIS" Tokens.THIS
      | "throw" => kw "THROW" Tokens.THROW
      | "throws" => kw "THROWS" Tokens.THROWS
      | "transient" => kw "TRANSIENT" Tokens.TRANSIENT
      | "try" => kw "TRY" Tokens.TRY
      | "void" => kw "VOID" Tokens.VOID
      | "volatile" => kw "VOLATILE" Tokens.VOLATILE
      | "while" => kw "WHILE" Tokens.WHILE
      | "_" => kw "UNDERSCORE" Tokens.UNDERSCORE
      | "true" => lit "TRUE" Tokens.TRUE
      | "false" => lit "FALSE" Tokens.FALSE
      | "null" => lit "NULL" Tokens.NULL
      | _ =>
          if List.exists (fn w => w = yytext) contextual_words then quiet_ident st (yypos, yytext)
          else stok_val st "IDENT" Markup.free (yypos, yytext, Tokens.IDENT, yytext)
    end

fun layout_newline st = Java_Layout.newline st

(* Accumulate one fragment of raw comment text, tagged with its own span. *)
fun push_raw_fragment st (yypos, yytext) =
    Java_Comments.push_raw st
      (yytext, Position.range_position
       (gp st yypos, gp st (yypos + String.size yytext)))

fun char_to_navi #"u" = Java_Ast.up
  | char_to_navi #"U" = Java_Ast.Up
  | char_to_navi #"r" = Java_Ast.right
  | char_to_navi #"d" = Java_Ast.down
  | char_to_navi c = raise Fail ("invalid navigation character " ^ String.str c)

(* Splits the text matched after the leading "@" into the tag name, its optional
   bracketed navigation string, and its optional parenthesized level. *)
fun parse_tag_navi_level text =
    let
      val trim =
        Substring.string o Substring.dropr Char.isSpace o Substring.full
      val after_at =
        Substring.dropl (fn c => c = #"@" orelse Char.isSpace c)
          (Substring.full text)
      val (name_ss, rest1) =
        Substring.splitl (fn c => Char.isAlphaNum c orelse c = #"_") after_at
      val rest1' = Substring.dropl Char.isSpace rest1
      val (navi, rest2) =
        if Substring.size rest1' > 0 andalso Substring.sub (rest1', 0) =
          #"[" then
          let
            val (navi_ss, after) =
              Substring.splitl (fn c => c <> #"]") (Substring.triml 1 rest1')
          in (map char_to_navi (String.explode (Substring.string navi_ss)),
              Substring.triml 1 after) end
        else ([], rest1')
      val rest2' = Substring.dropl Char.isSpace rest2
      val level =
        if Substring.size rest2' > 0 andalso Substring.sub (rest2', 0) =
          #"(" then
          let val (lvl_ss, _) =
            Substring.splitl (fn c => c <> #")") (Substring.triml 1 rest2')
          in valOf (Int.fromString (Substring.string lvl_ss)) end
        else 0
    in (trim (Substring.string name_ss), navi, level) end

(* A bodyless tag is a complete antiquotation with the empty body - but only
   where the comment opener allowed it ("//@", "/*@"): elsewhere it is plain
   comment text and is dropped here. *)
fun antiq_flush_pending_tag st =
    let val (name, navi, level, pos) = !(antiq_tag st) in
      if name = "" then ()
      else
        (antiq_tag st := ("", [], 0, Position.none);
         if !(bodyless_ok st)
         then Java_Comments.push_antiquotation
           st {tag = (name, pos)} navi {level = level} {cartouche = ("", pos)}
         else ())
    end

fun antiq_tag_seen st (yypos, yytext) =
    let
      val (name, navi, level) = parse_tag_navi_level yytext
      val _ = if yypos = !(comment_start st) then bodyless_ok st := true else ()
    in antiq_flush_pending_tag st; antiq_tag
      st := (name, navi, level, gp st yypos) end

fun antiq_body_start st yypos = antiq_start st := gp st yypos

fun antiq_body_push st text = antiq_buf st := text :: !(antiq_buf st)

fun antiq_body_finish st yypos =
    let
      val body_text = String.concat (rev (!(antiq_buf st)))
      val body_pos = Position.range_position (!(antiq_start st), gp st yypos)
      val (tag_text, navi, level, tag_pos) = !(antiq_tag st)
    in
      antiq_buf st := [];
      antiq_tag st := ("", [], 0, Position.none);
      Java_Comments.push_antiquotation
        st {tag = (tag_text, tag_pos)} navi {level = level} {cartouche =
            (body_text, body_pos)}
    end

(* The quoted-string alternative to a cartouche body: tag, level and body in one
   rule. *)
fun antiq_tag_and_string_seen st (yypos, yytext) =
    let
      fun find_quote i = if String.sub (yytext, i) =
        #"\"" then i else find_quote (i + 1)
      val qpos = find_quote 0
      val (name, navi, level) =
        parse_tag_navi_level (String.substring (yytext, 0, qpos))
      val body_len = String.size yytext - qpos - 2
      val body_text = String.substring (yytext, qpos + 1, body_len)
      val body_start = yypos + qpos + 1
      val body_pos =
        Position.range_position
          (gp st body_start, gp st (body_start + body_len))
    in
      Java_Comments.push_antiquotation
        st {tag = (name, gp st yypos)} navi {level = level} {cartouche =
            (body_text, body_pos)}
    end

fun tag_with_body st (yypos, yytext) =
    (report st (yypos, String.size yytext, Markup.antiquote,
                "Java antiquotation tag+body", "");
     antiq_tag_and_string_seen st (yypos, yytext))

fun tag_only st (yypos, yytext) =
    (antiq_tag_seen st (yypos, yytext);
     if !(bodyless_ok st)
     then report st (yypos, String.size yytext, Markup.antiquote,
                     "Java antiquotation tag", "")
     else ())

fun cartouche_open st (yypos, yytext) block =
    (report st (yypos, String.size yytext, Markup.cartouche,
                "Java antiquotation body", "");
     antiq_depth st := 1; antiq_from_block st := block;
     antiq_body_start st (yypos + String.size yytext))


(* A comment opens: "//" runs to the end of the line, "/*" to the next "*/"
   (block comments do not nest in Go). *)
fun comment_open st block yypos =
    (bodyless_ok st := false; comment_start st := yypos + 2;
     comment_depth st := (if block then 1 else 0);
     antiq_from_block st := block)

(* The first lexical error is recorded (see "Java_State"); the lexer
   carries on, and "Java_Parse.parse" reports it when the parse is over. *)
fun lex_fail (st : arg) (msg, pos) =
    (case !(#lex_error st) of NONE =>
     #lex_error st := SOME (msg, pos) | SOME _ => ())

(* Shadows the default "eof" of the "[state]" header: an unterminated
   antiquotation is a genuine error, reported at its own start. *)
fun eof (st : arg) =
    let
      val _ =
        if !(antiq_depth st) > 0
        then (antiq_depth st := 0;
              lex_fail st ("Unterminated antiquotation body: no matching " ^
                           "closing cartouche before the end of input",
                           !(antiq_start st)))
        else antiq_flush_pending_tag st
    in Tokens.EOF (Position.none, Position.none) end
\<close>
lex_definitions\<open>
D=[0-9];
DU=[0-9_];
H=[0-9A-Fa-f];
HU=[0-9A-Fa-f_];
O=[0-7];
NA=[^\000-\127];
LET=([A-Za-z_$]|{NA});
LD=([A-Za-z0-9_$]|{NA});
EXPN=[eE][+\-]?{D}{DU}*;
FSUF=[fFdD];
INTLIT=((0[xX]{HU}+|0[bB][01_]+|{D}{DU}*)[lL]?);
HEXFLOAT=(0[xX]({HU}*"."?{HU}*)[pP][+\-]?{D}{DU}*{FSUF}?);
HWS=[\ \t];
WS=[\ \t\r\012];
NAVI=[uUrd];
ATAG={HWS}*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*({HWS}*"["{NAVI}*"]")?({HWS}*"("{D}+")")?;
ATAG={ATAG};
ES=(\\(["\\abfnrtv]|x{H}{H}));
